WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.566
Air leak: the time you have, and how to
use it.

2
00:00:04.816 --> 00:00:10.088
August twenty eighteen. On the station,
the pressure starts to drop, very slowly.

3
00:00:10.128 --> 00:00:13.246
Ground teams notice it first, while the
crew sleeps.

4
00:00:14.146 --> 00:00:17.030
The cause: a hole just two millimeters
wide,

5
00:00:17.030 --> 00:00:19.915
in a Soyuz spacecraft docked to the
station.

6
00:00:20.815 --> 00:00:23.264
The leak was slow, so the crew had time.

7
00:00:23.304 --> 00:00:28.093
The key number is the reserve time: how
long until the air becomes unsafe.

8
00:00:28.993 --> 00:00:30.576
First, gather the crew.

9
00:00:30.616 --> 00:00:35.460
Then close the hatches, module by module,
to find where the air escapes.

10
00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:40.841
In twenty eighteen, the crew found the
hole, and sealed it with gauze and epoxy.

11
00:00:41.741 --> 00:00:47.163
Stay calm and follow the numbers: a small
leak usually leaves hours, not seconds.

12
00:00:47.203 --> 00:00:50.466
Close each hatch calmly, then check the
pressure again.

13
00:00:51.366 --> 00:00:54.486
Practice it in the depressurization
simulation.

